ed; it does **not** forbid intermediate values of \\(k\\).\n\n6. **A stronger restriction using the structure of the triangle.**  \n   Consider the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{a+b=s\\}\\) for \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can contain **all** points of at most one diagonal (if it is\n   parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)); otherwise it meets each diagonal in at most one point.\n   Hence, after using the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, at least one diagonal must\n   remain uncovered completely unless all non\u2011sunny lines are parallel to\n   \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n   *If any non\u2011sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)*, then at most one point\n   of each diagonal can be covered by that line, so the total number of points\n   covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)n\\).  \n   Adding the maximal contribution \\(kn\\) of the sunny lines gives at most\n   \\(n^2\\) points, which is **strictly smaller** than \\(|S_n|\\) for every\n   \\(n\\ge 3\\) when \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Hence this case is impossible.\n\n   *Therefore every non\u2011sunny line must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).*  \n   Such a line is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with an integer \\(c\\).  \n   Distinct lines correspond to distinct values of \\(c\\).  \n   To cover all points of \\(S_n\\) we need the lines  \n\n   \\[\n      x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   This uses exactly \\(n\\) lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\).  Consequently any solution\n   with \\(k\\ge 1\\) must have **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny.\n\n7. **Realisation of the two admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Each line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence not sunny) and together they cover\n   every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   For each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) choose a distinct sunny line passing\n   through that point (for instance the line with slope \\(a\\) and intercept\n   \\(b-a\\)).  Because the lines are all dif