and are covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers \\(T_{n+1}\\), and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is any sunny line that passes through the point \\(P_{n+1}\\).  \nFor concreteness we may take  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-1 = 2\\,(x-(n+1))\\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor undefined nor \\(-1\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) contains \\(P_{n+1}\\), all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered (the old points by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), the new point by \\(\\ell\\)).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines and exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Validity of the induction  \n\nThe two constructions above show that, assuming the existence of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) for every \\(k\\le n\\), we obtain \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) for every \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) for every \\(k\\le n\\).  \nHence for the integer \\(n+1\\) we obtain families with **all** numbers of sunny lines from \\(0\\) up to \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) supplies families \\(\\mathcal L_{3,k}\\) for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nApplying the inductive step repeatedly, we obtain for each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) families \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"

