\dots ,k).\n     \\]\n\n     Each \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\) (therefore sunny) and meets the column \\(x=t+i\\) in the point \\((t+i,i+j-1)\\).  \n     Because \\(1\\le i,j\\le k\\), the ordinate \\(i+j-1\\) always belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); consequently every point of \\(S\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   * Altogether we have \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\); the total number of lines is \\(t+k=n\\) and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n4. **Construction for the case \\(k=n\\) with odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 1\\)).  \n   For each \\(r\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_r:\\; y = x + r .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope is \\(1\\) (sunny) and the intercept \\(r\\) is an integer.  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a=r\\) for a unique \\(r\\) in the above interval, because  \n\n   \\[\n   -(m)\\le b-a\\le m\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n,\\;a+b\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(2m+1=n\\) lines \\(\\ell_r\\).  \n   All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   When \\(n\\) is even, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{n}{2}\\).  \n   A sunny line therefore contains at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   With \\(n\\) sunny lines we could cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points, whereas \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{n^{2}}2+\\frac n2\\).  \n   The deficit \\(\\frac n2>0\\) shows that covering all points with \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible when \\(n\\) is even.\n\nAll the statements above are proved rigorously; therefore the description of \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathcal K_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]