,2),\\qquad P_3=(3,1)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(which belong to \\(S_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \nThe lines \\(P_1P_2\\) (slope \\(1\\)), \\(P_1P_3\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) and \\(P_2P_3\\) (slope \\(-2\\)) are all sunny.  \nSince a line can contain at most two of the three points, at least three distinct lines are needed to cover them, contradicting the assumption of only two sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Covering with \\(m\\) sunny lines for \\(m\\ge 3\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains the whole set  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny, and the lines are distinct because the slopes are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=a-b+m\\quad\\bigl(1\\le i\\le m\\bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) give \\(a-b\\ge 1-m\\), so \\(i\\ge1\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) yields \\(a-b\\le m-1\\), so \\(i\\le m\\).  \nA direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\):\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i+1}a+\\frac{1}{i+1}\n\\iff (i+1)b=ia+1\n\\iff ia+1=(i+1)b\n\\iff a-b=m-i,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly (5).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  Therefore \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}L_i\\supseteq T_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Covering the whole set \\(S_n\\) with a prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition (1) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Construction.*  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  None of them is sunny and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on the line whose first coordinate equals its \\(x\\)–value.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with any sunny line passing through the single uncovered point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge 3\\).*  \nWrite \\(k=m\\) with \\(m\\ge3\\).  \n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-m).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct and non‑sunny.  \n2. **Sunny part.**  By Lemma 2 there exist \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) covering the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Translate each \\(L_i\\) by the vector \\((n-m,0)\\); denote the translated lines by \\(\\widetilde L_i\\).  \n   Translation does not change slopes, so the \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) remain sunny and distinct.  \n   By Lemma 2 (applied after the translation) the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\widetilde L_i\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge n-m+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the part of \\(S_n\\) that is not covered by the vertical lines (6).\n\n3. **Full family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-m}\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_{\\,m}\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(m=k\\) of which are sunny, and by construction every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to some \\(V_j\\) (if its first coordinate is \\(\\le n-m\\)) or to some \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) (otherwise).  Hence the covering condition holds.\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\neq2\\) we have exhibited a suitable configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Non‑existence for \\(k=2\\))\n\nThe statement of Lemma 1 already shows that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma 3 (existence for all \\(k\\neq2\\)) with Lemma 4 (non‑existence for \\(k=2\\)) we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nAll other lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are non‑sunny, i.e. vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(2,2),\\qquad P_3=(3,1),\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhich belong to \\(S_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThe lines determined by the pairs are  \n\n\\[\nP_1P_2:\\;y=x\\quad(\\text{slope }1),\\qquad\nP_1P_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\quad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12),\\qquad\nP_2P_3:\\;y=-2x+5\\quad(\\text{slope }-2).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny.  \nSince a line can contain at most two of the three points, covering the set \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\) requires at least three distinct lines.  Consequently any covering of \\(S_n\\) needs at least three sunny lines, and the case \\(k=2\\) is impossible. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Covering a triangular lattice with \\(m\\) sunny lines (\\(m\\ge3\\))\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge3\\) define, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=a-b+m .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we have \\(a-b\\ge 1-m\\), whence \\(i\\ge1\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) gives \\(a-b\\le m-1\\), whence \\(i\\le m\\).  Thus \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\).\n\nA short computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\):\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i+1}a+\\frac{1}{i+1}\n\\iff (i+1)b=ia+1\n\\iff a-b=m-i,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly (15).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}L_i\\supseteq T_m .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) cover the whole set \\(T_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\neq2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nNone of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*If \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with any sunny line passing through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n*If \\(k\\ge3\\).*  \nWrite \\(k=m\\) with \\(m\\ge3\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny vertical lines.**  \n   For \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-m\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j .\n   \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-m\\) lines are distinct and non‑sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny covering of the residual triangle.**  \n   By Lemma 2 there exist \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) covering  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n   \\tag{19}\n   \\]\n\n   Translate each \\(L_i\\) by the vector \\((n-m,0)\\); denote the translated lines by \\(\\widetilde L_i\\).  \n   Translation does not affect slopes, so the \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) are still sunny and remain distinct.  \n   The translation sends the set \\(T_m\\) onto  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge n-m+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\tag{20}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) that are not covered by the vertical lines (18).\n\n3. **Full family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-m}\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_{\\,m}\\}.\n   \\tag{21}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(m=k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) either has first coordinate \\(\\le n-m\\) (hence lies on some \\(V_j\\)) or belongs to \\(T_m'\\) (hence lies on some \\(\\widetilde L_i\\)).  Therefore the covering condition is satisfied.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have exhibited a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non‑existence for \\(k=2\\)\n\nLemma 1 shows that any covering of \\(S_n\\) needs either \\(0,1\\) or at least \\(3\\) sunny lines.  Hence the value \\(k=2\\) cannot be realized for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
