ins the point \\((1,1)\\) as its left\u2011side intersection, its slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), which is not sunny.  \n   If a line contains \\((1,1)\\) but its left\u2011side intersection is \\((1,i)\\) with \\(i>1\\), the line is vertical, also not sunny.  \n   Hence at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n   The argument fails for \\(n=3\\) because the three points on the left side can be paired with the three points on the bottom side in a way that avoids the above obstruction; indeed the explicit construction for \\(k=3\\) shows that \\(k=n\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the base case, the inductive construction for \\(k\\le n-1\\), and the impossibility proof for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\) yields the complete description of all admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\,(n,1),\\,(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the slope of the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following families of three distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly the indicated number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (equations) | Sunny? |\n|------|-------------------|--------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) | only the last line |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;y=-2x+5\\) | the last two |\n| 3 | \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;y=-2x+5\\) | all three |\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive construction for \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Inductive step)\n\n*Assume* that for some \\(m\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n*Then* for the same integer \\(k\\) (now with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\)) there exist \\(m+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(m\\) lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (such a configuration exists by the hypothesis).  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{new}}:\\;x+y=m+2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \n\nThe set difference \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\setminus\\mathcal P_m\\) consists precisely of the \\(m+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,m+1),\\;(2,m),\\dots ,\\;(m+1,1),\n\\]\n\nall of which satisfy \\(x+y=m+2\\) and therefore lie on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\).  \nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\cup\\{L_{\\text{new}}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\).  \nSince the points of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) with sum \\(m+2\\) do not belong to \\(\\mathcal P_m\\), the new line is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) equals the number in \\(\\mathcal L\\), namely \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 repeatedly, starting from the base case \\(n=3\\), we obtain for **every** integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1\n\\]\n\na family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Non\u2011existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 4\\). In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) at least one line is **not** sunny; consequently \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the left side of the triangle, i.e. the set  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA non\u2011vertical line meets \\(L\\) in at most one point, because two distinct points of \\(L\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nSince a sunny line is, by definition, not vertical, each sunny line meets \\(L\\) in **at most** one point.\n\nAnalogously, the bottom side  \n\n\\[\nB:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nis intersected by a non\u2011horizontal line in at most one point; a sunny line is not horizontal, hence each sunny line meets \\(B\\) in at most one point.\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) points on \\(L\\) and \\(n\\) points on \\(B\\).  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then the \\(n\\) intersections with \\(L\\) would have to be distinct (otherwise some point of \\(L\\) would be missed) and likewise the \\(n\\) intersections with \\(B\\) would have to be distinct.  \nTherefore every sunny line would be uniquely determined by a pair  \n\n\\[\n(1,i)\\in L,\\qquad (j,1)\\in B,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(i,j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); its equation is the line through these two points.\n\nNow examine the point \\((1,1)\\).  \nIf a sunny line uses \\((1,1)\\) as its left\u2011side intersection, then its second intersection with \\(B\\) must be \\((1,1)\\) as well, because the line through \\((1,1)\\) and any other point of \\(B\\) is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), which is forbidden.  \nHence a sunny line cannot have \\((1,1)\\) as its left\u2011side intersection.\n\nIf a sunny line contains \\((1,1)\\) **but** its left\u2011side intersection is some other point \\((1,i)\\) with \\(i>1\\), then the line is vertical (both points have \\(x=1\\)), which is also forbidden.\n\nConsequently the point \\((1,1)\\) cannot belong to any sunny line, contradicting the covering requirement.  \nThus at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the above obstruction disappears: the three points of \\(L\\) and \\(B\\) can be paired as \\((1,1)\\leftrightarrow(1,1),\\;(1,2)\\leftrightarrow(3,1),\\;(1,3)\\leftrightarrow(2,1)\\); the resulting three lines have slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), all sunny. Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final classification of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the explicit constructions of Section\u202f2 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) is possible.\n\n* For any \\(n\\ge 4\\) Lemma\u202f1 guarantees the existence of configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n  Lemma\u202f2 proves that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]"

