            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,1),\\qquad B=(n,1),\\qquad C=(1,n).\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad \\infty\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad -1\\quad(\\text{parallel to }x+y=0).\n\\]\n\nAll other slopes are called *sunny*.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Configurations\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of three distinct lines | sunny lines |\n|------|--------------------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) | the last line |\n| 3 | \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;y=-2x+5\\) | all three |\n\n*Verification.*  \nEach line contains only points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\); a direct inspection shows that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines.  \nThe slopes of the three lines in the third row are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.  \nIn the second row the only sunny line is the one with slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that three distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) contain exactly two sunny lines.  \nBecause a sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\), each sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) (any two points sharing the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate, the same \\(y\\)‑coordinate or the same sum would force a forbidden slope).  \nHence the two sunny lines together cover at most four points, leaving at least two points to be covered by the remaining (non‑sunny) line.  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); each of these can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).  \nA short case analysis (checking all possibilities for the non‑sunny line) shows that no choice can cover the two points left uncovered by the sunny lines.  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\nConsequently, for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A configuration for \\(n=4\\) with \\(k=2\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_4=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le5\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains ten points.  \nThe following four distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: x=1 \\qquad\\text{(vertical, non‑sunny)},\\\\\nL_2 &: x+y=4 \\qquad\\text{(slope }-1\\text{, non‑sunny)},\\\\\nL_3 &: y=x-1 \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, sunny)},\\\\\nL_4 &: y=-2x+7 \\qquad\\text{(slope }-2\\text{, sunny)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \nThe points covered by each line are listed below.\n\n* \\(L_1\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\\);\n* \\(L_2\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(L_3\\) passes through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,2)\\);\n* \\(L_4\\) passes through \\((2,3)\\) and \\((4,1)\\).\n\nCollecting the eight distinct points we obtain exactly the ten points of \\(\\mathcal P_4\\); each point appears at least once.  \nThe slopes of \\(L_3\\) and \\(L_4\\) are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), both sunny; the other two lines are non‑sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for \\(n=4\\).\n\nTogether with the families obtained from the \\(n=3\\) constructions by adding the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=5\\) we have, for \\(n=4\\), configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive construction (Lemma 1)\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(m\\ge3\\). Suppose there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le m\\)).  \n> Then the family\n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\,L_{\\text{new}}\\,\\},\\qquad\n> L_{\\text{new}}:\\;x+y=m+2,\n> \\]\n> consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) has slope \\(-1\\); hence it is non‑sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) either belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) (and is therefore covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)) or satisfies \\(x+y=m+2\\) (and lies on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\)).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\).  \nBecause \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) is non‑sunny, the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) equals that in \\(\\mathcal L\\), namely \\(k\\).  \nFinally, none of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can coincide with \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\): every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le m+1\\), whereas every point of \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=m+2\\).  Hence the two families are disjoint and \\(\\mathcal L'\\) consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines. ∎\n\nApplying Lemma 1 repeatedly, starting from the explicit configurations for \\(n=4\\) (which exist for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le3\\)), we obtain for **every** integer \\(n\\ge4\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1\n\\]\n\na family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\) (Lemma 2)\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge4\\). In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) at least one line is non‑sunny; consequently \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two legs of the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_n\\):\n\\[\nL:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nB:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal; therefore it meets \\(L\\) in **at most one** point and meets \\(B\\) in **at most one** point.  \nSince \\(|L|=|B|=n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must meet each of the sets \\(L\\) and \\(B\\) in **exactly** one point per line; otherwise some point of \\(L\\) (or \\(B\\)) would remain uncovered.\n\nHence each of the \\(n\\) lines is uniquely determined by a pair  \n\n\\[\n(1,i)\\in L,\\qquad (j,1)\\in B,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(i,j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIf a line contains the point \\((1,1)\\) as its intersection with \\(L\\), then its second intersection with \\(B\\) must also be \\((1,1)\\); the line is therefore horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), which is not sunny.  \nIf a line contains \\((1,1)\\) as its intersection with \\(B\\), then its second intersection with \\(L\\) is also \\((1,1)\\); the line is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), also not sunny.  \n\nThus at least one of the \\(n\\) lines cannot be sunny. ∎\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the argument above fails because the three points of \\(L\\) and \\(B\\) can be paired as \\((1,1)\\leftrightarrow(1,1),\\;(1,2)\\leftrightarrow(3,1),\\;(1,3)\\leftrightarrow(2,1)\\); the resulting three lines are all sunny (see the construction for \\(k=3\\) in Section 2). Hence the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) is sharp for \\(n\\ge4\\) and does not hold for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final classification\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible numbers are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth statements follow from the explicit constructions of Sections 2–4 and the impossibility result of Lemma 2. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
