"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **All\u2011sunny configuration.**  \n   By induction on \\(n\\) we construct, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\), a family  \n   \\(\\mathcal S_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) *sunny* lines that covers all points  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The induction step adds two new sunny lines to a configuration for \\(n-2\\) and\n   shows that the new outer layer of points (those with \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\))\n   is completely covered.\n\n2. **From all\u2011sunny to arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Take the first \\(k\\) lines of the all\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_n\\);* they are\n   sunny and together cover exactly the points of the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining points are those with \\(b>k\\).  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) we add the horizontal line\n   \\(y=j\\).  These \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and each\n   contains all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(j\\).  \n   Consequently the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n   the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, giving a configuration with exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines.\n\n   Since the construction works for every \\(k\\), all integers\n   \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(m\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_m=|T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  An all\u2011sunny covering for every \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove by induction that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines such that  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Basis \\(n=3\\)\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=x      &&\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\\nL_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 &&\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_3 &: y=-2x+5 &&\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the\nsix points of \\(T_3\\) are exactly the six points listed above, so (2.1) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Induction step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 5\\) a family \\(\\mathcal S_{\\,n-2}\\) of\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\) is already constructed.\nWe shall enlarge it to a family \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering\n\\(T_n\\).\n\nThe set difference \\(T_n\\setminus T_{\\,n-2}\\) consists of the two outer\n\u201cborder\u201d layers  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_1 &=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n\\},\\\\\nB_2 &=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(B_1\\) and \\(B_2\\) are arithmetic progressions of lattice points:\n\\[\nB_1=\\{(1,n-1),(2,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,1)\\},\n\\qquad\nB_2=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine two new lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{n-1}&:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{n}{2}+ \\frac12 ,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{n}   &:\\; y = -2x + n+2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\n\\(L_{n-1},L_n\\) are sunny and distinct from each other and from the lines of\n\\(\\mathcal S_{\\,n-2}\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(B_2\\).*  \nFor every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,\\; -\\tfrac12 a+\\tfrac{n}{2}+\\tfrac12\\bigr)\n      =\\bigl(a,\\; n+1-a\\bigr)\\in B_2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_{n-1}\\) contains **all** points of \\(B_2\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(B_1\\).*  \nFor every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,\\; -2a+n+2\\bigr)=\\bigl(a,\\; n-a\\bigr)\\in B_1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(L_{n}\\) contains **all** points of \\(B_1\\).\n\nConsequently the two new lines together cover the whole set\n\\(T_n\\setminus T_{\\,n-2}\\).  Adding them to the already existing\n\\(\\mathcal S_{\\,n-2}\\) yields a family \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) of \\(n\\) sunny lines\nthat satisfies (2.1).  This completes the induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 sunny part.*  \nTake the first \\(k\\) lines of the all\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) constructed\nabove; denote them by \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \nBecause the construction of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) was performed by repeatedly\nadding the two outer layers, the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) already cover the\ninner triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 non\u2011sunny part.*  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) introduce the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct, have slope \\(0\\) (hence are **not** sunny), and\neach of them contains precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate\nequals \\(j\\).  The collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{\\,n-k}= \\{H_{k+1},H_{k+2},\\dots ,H_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-k\\) lines.\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 covering.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs either to \\(T_k\\) (so it lies on one of\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\)) or has \\(b\\ge k+1\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line\n\\(H_b\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\Bigr)\\cup\n           \\Bigl(\\bigcup_{j=k+1}^{n}H_j\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines,\nall distinct.  Therefore a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown\n\n* for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct sunny\n  lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* using that configuration together with horizontal lines we can realise any\n  prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
