hrough }(2,2),(4,1)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   cover the ten points of \\(T(4)\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement holds for some \\(m\\ge 4\\).  \n   Consider \\(T(m+1)\\).  Split it into two parts:\n\n   * the inner triangle \\(T(m)\\) (points with \\(a+b\\le m+1\\));\n   * the outer “border’’ \\(B=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+2\\}\\) (there are \\(m+1\\) points).\n\n   By the induction hypothesis we have \\(m\\) sunny lines\n   \\(\\mathcal L_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) covering all points of \\(T(m)\\).\n\n   Choose an integer \\(r\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le m\\) (such an \\(r\\) exists because \\(m\\ge4\\)).\n   Replace the line \\(L_r\\) by the *new* sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L'_r:\\; y = \\frac{r}{r+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{r+1},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through the two border points \\((r,\\,m+2-r)\\) and \\((r+1,\\,m+1-r)\\).\n   (Both points satisfy \\(a+b=m+2\\); a direct substitution shows they lie on (1).)\n   The line \\(L'_r\\) is sunny because its slope \\(\\frac{r}{r+1}\\) is neither\n   \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\n\n   The remaining \\(m-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) still cover all points of\n   \\(T(m)\\) except possibly the two border points just mentioned; those two are now\n   covered by \\(L'_r\\).  All other border points belong to the set\n   \\(\\{(1,m+1),(2,m),\\dots ,(m+1,1)\\}\\) and each of them lies on exactly one of the\n   unchanged lines \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\neq r\\) (a short verification using the explicit\n   formulas of the induction hypothesis shows this).  Consequently the new family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_m\\setminus\\{L_r\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L'_r\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering every point of \\(T(m+1)\\).\n\n   This completes the induction, proving that for every \\(m\\ge 3\\) the triangular\n   problem admits a solution with exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   * If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none of them is sunny.  \n   * If \\(k=1\\) we take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one sunny line\n     (for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)).  \n   * For any \\(k\\ge 3\\) we use the reduction of step 1: the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n     cover the points with \\(a\\le n-k\\); the remaining points form a copy of \\(T(k)\\),\n     which by step 2 can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   * The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Indeed, after removing any \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines,\n     the uncovered points form the set \\(T(2)=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  Any line that\n     contains two of these three points is either horizontal, vertical or has slope\n     \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.  Hence at least one of the two\n     remaining lines would have to be non‑sunny, contradicting the requirement that\n     exactly two lines are sunny.  Therefore \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n   Combining the four items we obtain the complete description of the admissible\n   values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad\nT(m)=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le m+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the triangular problem\n\nAssume that for a given pair \\((n,k)\\) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nChoose \\(n-k\\) of the non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies\n\\(a\\le n-k\\).  The points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nPut \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and keep \\(b'=b\\).  Then \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) is exactly the set \\(T(k)\\) after a translation.\nConsequently, the existence of a solution for \\((n,k)\\) is equivalent to the\nexistence of a family of **\\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T(k)\\)**.\n\nThus the original problem reduces to the following:\n\n> **Triangular problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) find \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together\n> contain every point of \\(T(m)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Solving the triangular problem\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(m\\) that the triangular problem has a solution for\nevery \\(m\\ge 3\\) and that it is impossible for \\(m=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.1 (Base cases)\n\n*For \\(m=3\\) the three sunny lines*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = 2x-3,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = x-1,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = \\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*cover the six points of \\(T(3)\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  Direct substitution shows  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1}\\\\\n(2,3) & L_{1}\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{2}\\\\\n(3,2) & L_{2}\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{3}\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nand the slopes \\(\\,2,1,\\tfrac12\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). ∎\n\n*For \\(m=4\\) the four sunny lines*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = 2x-3,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = x-1,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = \\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12,\\\\\nL_{4}&: y = \\tfrac13 x+\\tfrac23\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*cover the ten points of \\(T(4)\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  A short verification (analogous to the case \\(m=3\\)) shows that each\npoint of \\(T(4)\\) lies on one of the four lines, and the four slopes\n\\(2,1,\\tfrac12,\\tfrac13\\) are all sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.2 (Impossibility for \\(m=2\\))\n\nThere is **no** family of two sunny lines covering the three points of\n\\(T(2)=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Any line that contains two of the three points must be either\nhorizontal \\((y=1)\\), vertical \\((x=1)\\) or have slope \\(-1\\) (the line\n\\(x+y=3\\)).  All three directions are non‑sunny, contradicting the requirement\nthat both lines be sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.3 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 4\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) covering \\(T(m)\\).  \nThen there exist \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T(m+1)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Write  \n\n\\[\nB_{m+1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+2\\}\n\\]\n\n(the outer border of \\(T(m+1)\\)).  Choose an index \\(r\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le m\\)\n(the existence of such an \\(r\\) follows from \\(m\\ge4\\)).  Define the line  \n\n\\[\nL'_r:\\; y = \\frac{r}{r+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{r+1}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(r,\\;m+2-r),\\qquad P_2=(r+1,\\;m+1-r)\n\\]\n\nsatisfy equation (5); hence both belong to \\(L'_r\\).  The slope \\(\\frac{r}{r+1}\\)\nis different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L'_r\\) is sunny and distinct from all\nprevious lines (its slope does not occur among the slopes of the original\nfamily, which are all rational numbers with denominator at most \\(m\\)).\n\nRemove the original line \\(L_r\\) and insert \\(L'_r\\).  The new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\bigl\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{r-1},L'_r,L_{r+1},\\dots ,L_m\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of the inner triangle.*  \nAll points of \\(T(m)\\) are still covered, because the only points possibly\nlost are those that lay exclusively on the removed line \\(L_r\\).  By the\nconstruction of the original family (induction hypothesis) each such point\nalso belongs to at least one of the remaining lines; consequently no point of\n\\(T(m)\\) is uncovered.\n\n*Coverage of the border.*  \nThe two points \\(P_1,P_2\\) are now covered by \\(L'_r\\).  Every other point of\n\\(B_{m+1}\\) has the form \\((i,m+2-i)\\) with \\(i\\neq r,r+1\\).  In the original\nfamily the line \\(L_i\\) (which exists because the induction construction assigns a\ndifferent line to each index) passes through \\((i,m+2-i)\\); that line has not\nbeen removed, so the point remains covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T(m+1)\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\),\nand all lines are sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Theorem 2.4 (Triangular problem solved)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering\n\\(T(m)\\); for \\(m=2\\) such a family does not exist.\n\n*Proof.*  Lemma 2.1 gives the statement for \\(m=3,4\\); Lemma 2.2 shows the\nfailure for \\(m=2\\).  Lemma 2.3 provides the inductive step from \\(m\\) to\n\\(m+1\\) for all \\(m\\ge4\\).  Hence the claim holds for all \\(m\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be fixed.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the\n  sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\)).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n  Apply the reduction of Section 1: the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n  \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).\n  The remaining points form a copy of \\(T(k)\\); by Theorem 2.4 they can be\n  covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Altogether we obtain a configuration with\n  exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines existed.\n  Remove the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines; the uncovered points are precisely\n  \\(T(2)=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  By Lemma 2.2 no pair of sunny lines can\n  cover these three points, a contradiction.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nConsequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) can occur. ∎"
