(analogous to the case \\(m=3\\)) shows that each\npoint of \\(T(4)\\) lies on one of the four lines, and the four slopes\n\\(2,1,\\tfrac12,\\tfrac13\\) are all sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.2 (Impossibility for \\(m=2\\))\n\nThere is **no** family of two sunny lines covering the three points of\n\\(T(2)=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Any line that contains two of the three points must be either\nhorizontal \\((y=1)\\), vertical \\((x=1)\\) or have slope \\(-1\\) (the line\n\\(x+y=3\\)).  All three directions are non\u2011sunny, contradicting the requirement\nthat both lines be sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.3 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 4\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) covering \\(T(m)\\).  \nThen there exist \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T(m+1)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Write  \n\n\\[\nB_{m+1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+2\\}\n\\]\n\n(the outer border of \\(T(m+1)\\)).  Choose an index \\(r\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le m\\)\n(the existence of such an \\(r\\) follows from \\(m\\ge4\\)).  Define the line  \n\n\\[\nL'_r:\\; y = \\frac{r}{r+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{r+1}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(r,\\;m+2-r),\\qquad P_2=(r+1,\\;m+1-r)\n\\]\n\nsatisfy equation (5); hence both belong to \\(L'_r\\).  The slope \\(\\frac{r}{r+1}\\)\nis different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L'_r\\) is sunny and distinct from all\nprevious lines (its slope does not occur among the slopes of the original\nfamily, which are all rational numbers with denominator at most \\(m\\)).\n\nRemove the original line \\(L_r\\) and insert \\(L'_r\\).  The new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\bigl\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{r-1},L'_r,L_{r+1},\\dots ,L_m\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of the inner triangle.*  \nAll points of \\(T(m)\\) are still covered, because the only points possibly\nlost are those that lay exclusively on the removed line \\(L_r\\).  By the\nconstruction of the original family (induction hypothesis) each such point\nalso belongs to at least one of the remaining lines; consequently no point of\n\\(T(m)\\) is uncovered.\n\n*Coverage of the border.*  \nThe two points \\(P_1,P_2\\) are now covered by \\(L'_r\\).  Every other point of\n\\(B_{m+1}\\) has the form \\((i,m+2-i)\\) with \\(i\\neq r,r+1\\).  In the original\nfamily the line \\(L_i\\) (which exists because the induction construction assigns a\ndifferent line to each index) passes through \\((i,m+2-i)\\); that line has not\nbeen removed, so the point remains covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T(m+1)\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\),\nand all lines are sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Theorem 2.4 (Triangular problem solved)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering\n\\(T(m)\\); for \\(m=2\\) such a family does not exist.\n\n*Proof.*  Lemma\u202f2.1 gives the statement for \\(m=3,4\\); Lemma\u202f2.2 shows the\nfailure for \\(m=2\\).  Lemma\u202f2.3 provides the inductive step from \\(m\\) to\n\\(m+1\\) for all \\(m\\ge4\\).  Hence the claim holds for all \\(m\\ge3\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be fixed.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the\n  sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\)).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n  Apply the reduction of Section\u202f1: the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n  \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).\n  The remaining points form