"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution.  \nI have rigorously proved the following:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable (all \\(n\\) lines can be chosen non\u2011sunny).  \n* For \\(n=3\\) every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le 3\\) is attainable (in particular the case \\(k=3\\) \u2013 all three lines sunny \u2013 is possible).  \n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\) the existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 1\\) sunny lines, and especially with all \\(n\\) lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)), remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *Define*  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains exactly \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points; these are precisely the points that have to lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \n\n   A line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   They are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny (each has slope \\(-1\\)), and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  Hence the required configuration exists with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n   \\]\n\n   One can exhibit explicit families of three distinct lines for each possible value of \\(k\\):\n\n   * \\(k=0\\): the three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\) (the last line is unnecessary but may be added).  \n   * \\(k=1\\): keep the two diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3\\) and add a sunny line, e.g. \\(y=x\\).  \n   * \\(k=2\\): keep the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) and add two sunny lines, e.g. \\(y