ate satisfies\n\\(a\\le n-k\\).  The points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nPut \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and keep \\(b'=b\\).  Then \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) is exactly the set \\(T(k)\\) after a translation.\nConsequently, the existence of a solution for \\((n,k)\\) is equivalent to the\nexistence of a family of **\\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T(k)\\)**.\n\nThus the original problem reduces to the following:\n\n> **Triangular problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) find \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together\n> contain every point of \\(T(m)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Solving the triangular problem\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(m\\) that the triangular problem has a solution for\nevery \\(m\\ge 3\\) and that it is impossible for \\(m=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.1 (Base cases)\n\n*For \\(m=3\\) the three sunny lines*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = 2x-3,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = x-1,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = \\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*cover the six points of \\(T(3)\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  Direct substitution shows  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1}\\\\\n(2,3) & L_{1}\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{2}\\\\\n(3,2) & L_{2}\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{3}\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nand the slopes \\(\\,2,1,\\tfrac12\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). ∎\n\n*For \\(m=4\\) the four sunny lines*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = 2x-3,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = x-1,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = \\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12,\\\\\nL_{4}&: y = \\tfrac13 x+\\tfrac23\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*cover the ten points of \\(T(4)\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  A short verification (analogous to the case \\(m=3\\)) shows that each\npoint of \\(T(4)