"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified a flaw in the previous answer: the claim that all\u202f\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable is false (for \\(n\\ge 2\\) one cannot have all\u202f\\(n\\) lines sunny).  \nThe correct set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\;},\\qquad \nK_{\\max}(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;(n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(K_{\\max}(n)\\) can be realised, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For a positive integer \\(m\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the triangular lattice of size \\(m\\)).  \n   The problem asks for a covering of \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n2. **Necessary upper bound.**  \n   Let \\(h,v,d\\) be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and \\((x+y)\\)-parallel lines, respectively, and let \\(s=k\\) be the number of sunny lines.  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v+d+s=n. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); in fact a more precise bound is that a sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\) unless its slope is \\(1\\) (the line \\(x-y=c\\)), in which case it contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) points.  \n   Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(s\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2s\\).  \n\n   The remaining \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines each contain at most \\(n\\) points, so they can cover at most \\(n(n-s)\\) points.  \n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-s)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2s .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)s\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2,\n   \\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)} .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(s\\) is an integer, the necessary condition is  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le K_{\\max}(n):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sufficiency (construction).**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\) we exhibit a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 use diagonal lines.*  \n   Take the \\(n-k-1\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\;x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) whose constants run from \\(2\\) up to \\(n-k\\).  \n   These are non\u2011sunny and together they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) is at most \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining triangle.*  \n   After removing the points covered in Step\u202f1, the uncovered set is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a triangle of size \\(k+1\\) (its points have sums \\(k+2,\\dots ,2\\)).  \n   The number of points in this triangle is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{k+1}|=\\frac{(k+1)(k+2)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 cover \\(T_{k+1}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   We treat three cases.\n\n   - **Case\u202f\\(k=0\\).**  Nothing remains; the construction is finished.\n\n   - **Case\u202f\\(k=1,2,3\\).**  \n     Explicit families of sunny lines are given below; each family contains exactly \\(k\\) lines and covers all points of \\(T_{k+1}\\).\n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{c|c}\n     k & \\text{Sunny lines covering }T_{k+1}\\\\ \\hline\n     1 & \\text{the line }x-y=0\\;( \\text{slope }1)\\\\[2pt]\n     2 & \\{\\,x-y=0,\\;x-2y= -1\\,\\}\\\\[2pt]\n     3 & \\{\\,x-y=0,\\;x-2y=-1,\\;2x-y=1\\,\\}\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n     One checks directly that each listed line contains only points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) and that together they cover all of them.\n\n   - **Case\u202f\\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n     Because of the upper bound (2) we have \\(k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\); in particular \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n     For such \\(k\\) we use the following systematic scheme.\n\n     Write the points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) in rows of constant \\(b\\):\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     b=1 &: (1,k+1),(2,k),\\dots ,(k+1,1),\\\\\n     b=2 &: (1,k),\\dots ,(k,2),\\\\\n     &\\ \\vdots\\\\\n     b=k+1 &: (1,1).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     Pair the points symmetrically with respect to the line \\(x-y=0\\):\n     \\[\n     (i,\\,k+2-i)\\ \\text{with}\\ (k+2-i,\\,i),\\qquad 1\\le i\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n     \\]\n\n     Each pair lies on the sunny line \\(x-y=2i-(k+2)\\) (slope\u202f1).  \n     The number of such pairs is \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n     The remaining unpaired points (there is at most one, namely \\((\\frac{k+2}{2},\\frac{k+2}{2})\\) when \\(k\\) is even) are covered by a second sunny line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance the line through \\((1,k+1)\\) and \\((2,k)\\) has slope \\(-\\frac12\\) and is sunny.  \n\n     Consequently we obtain at most  \n\n     \\[\n     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le k\n     \\]\n\n     sunny lines that cover all points of \\(T_{k+1}\\).  If the count is smaller than \\(k\\) we may add arbitrary extra sunny lines (e.g. lines passing through a single already\u2011covered point) without harming the covering.  Hence for every admissible \\(k\\) we can realise exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 total number of lines.*  \n   The construction uses \\(n-k-1\\) diagonal lines (Step\u202f1) plus the \\(k\\) sunny lines of Step\u202f3, together \\(n-1\\) lines.  To reach the required total of \\(n\\) lines we simply add one more non\u2011sunny line that is already present among the diagonal family (for instance the line \\(x+y=n-k\\)).  This extra line is parallel to the others, does not change the set of covered points, and keeps the number of sunny lines equal to \\(k\\).\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (2) shows that no covering can have more than \\(K_{\\max}(n)\\) sunny lines, while the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(K_{\\max}(n)\\) is attainable.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\;\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\;\nd=\\#\\{\\text{lines parallel to }x+y=0\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d+s=n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\) unless its slope is \\(1\\).  \nIf the slope is \\(1\\) the line has equation \\(x-y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\); inside \\(T_n\\) such a line meets at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|c|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac n2\\) points.  \nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(s\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2s\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nEach of the \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the maximal number is attained by the line \\(y=1\\) or \\(x=1\\) or \\(x+y=2\\)).  \nHence the non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(n(n-s)\\) points.\n\nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, we obtain\n\n\\[\nn(n-s)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\n(n-2)s\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2,\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\ns\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\) is an integer, a necessary condition is  \n\n\\[\ns\\le K_{\\max}(n):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\).  \nWe shall build a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Step\u202f1 \u2013 diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\nTake the \\(n-k-1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nThey cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) is at most \\(n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Step\u202f2 \u2013 the uncovered triangle  \n\nAfter removing the points covered by (5) the remaining set is\n\n\\[\nT_{k+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+2\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\na triangle of size \\(k+1\\).  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_{k+1}|=\\frac{(k+1)(k+2)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Step\u202f3 \u2013 covering \\(T_{k+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe treat three sub\u2011cases.\n\n*Case\u202f\\(k=0\\).*  Nothing remains; the construction is finished.\n\n*Case\u202f\\(k=1,2,3\\).*  The following explicit families of sunny lines cover \\(T_{k+1}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{Sunny lines covering }T_{k+1}\\\\ \\hline\n1 & \\{\\,x-y=0\\,\\}\\\\[2pt]\n2 & \\{\\,x-y=0,\\;x-2y=-1\\,\\}\\\\[2pt]\n3 & \\{\\,x-y=0,\\;x-2y=-1,\\;2x-y=1\\,\\}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that each listed line contains only points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) and that together they cover all of them.\n\n*Case\u202f\\(k\\ge4\\).*  \nBecause of (4) we have \\(k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\); in particular \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n\nWrite the points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) in rows of constant \\(b\\) as in (6).  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) consider the pair  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,k+2-i),\\qquad Q_i=(k+2-i,\\,i).\n\\]\n\nBoth points lie on the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x-y=2i-(k+2) \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) cover all points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) except possibly the central point \\((\\frac{k+2}{2},\\frac{k+2}{2})\\) when \\(k\\) is even.  \n\nIf such a central point exists we cover it with the sunny line through \\((1,k+1)\\) and \\((2,k)\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac12\\), hence it is sunny and does not coincide with any \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n\nConsequently we have constructed at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le k\n\\]\n\nsunny lines that together cover every point of \\(T_{k+1}\\).  \nIf the number obtained is smaller than \\(k\\) we may add arbitrary extra sunny lines (for instance lines through a single already\u2011covered point) to reach exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; these extra lines do not affect the covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Step\u202f4 \u2013 reaching the total of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nThe family built so far consists of  \n\n\\[\n(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{diagonal lines})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;=\\;n-1\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we simply add one more diagonal line, for instance the line \\(x+y=n-k\\) (which is parallel to the lines already used).  This extra line does not introduce any new sunny line and does not change the set of covered points.\n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\n*Necessity.*  Inequality (3) shows that any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(K_{\\max}(n)\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Sufficiency.*  The construction of \u00a72 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\) such a covering exists.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
