ne\n1 & \\{\\,x-y=0\\,\\}\\\\[2pt]\n2 & \\{\\,x-y=0,\\;x-2y=-1\\,\\}\\\\[2pt]\n3 & \\{\\,x-y=0,\\;x-2y=-1,\\;2x-y=1\\,\\}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that each listed line contains only points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) and that together they cover all of them.\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge4\\).*  \nBecause of (4) we have \\(k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\); in particular \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n\nWrite the points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) in rows of constant \\(b\\) as in (6).  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) consider the pair  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,k+2-i),\\qquad Q_i=(k+2-i,\\,i).\n\\]\n\nBoth points lie on the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x-y=2i-(k+2) \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) cover all points of \\(T_{k+1}\\) except possibly the central point \\((\\frac{k+2}{2},\\frac{k+2}{2})\\) when \\(k\\) is even.  \n\nIf such a central point exists we cover it with the sunny line through \\((1,k+1)\\) and \\((2,k)\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac12\\), hence it is sunny and does not coincide with any \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n\nConsequently we have constructed at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le k\n\\]\n\nsunny lines that together cover every point of \\(T_{k+1}\\).  \nIf the number obtained is smaller than \\(k\\) we may add arbitrary extra sunny lines (for instance lines through a single already‑covered point) to reach exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; these extra lines do not affect the covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Step 4 – reaching the total of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nThe family built so far consists of  \n\n\\[\n(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{diagonal lines})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;=\\;n-1\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we simply add one more diagonal line, for instance the line \\(x+y=n-k\\) (which is parallel to the lines already used).  This extra line does not introduce any new sunny line and does not change the set of covered points.\n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\n*Necessity.*  Inequality (3) shows that any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(K_{\\max}(n)\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Sufficiency.*  The construction of §2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\) such a covering exists.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
